= The Barracks

The Reichswehr has not fought for Kapp, and it has not fought against him. Its officers sit in their barracks, and their men read the strike bulletins like everyone else. If the strike weakens, the generals may decide that Kapp is the winner after all.

- @soldiers: Send old comrades from the war to talk to the soldiers.
- @names: Print the names and addresses of the officers who went over to Kapp.

@soldiers
subtitle: Costs 1 fund. If the regiments elect councils again, the generals cannot move.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: We have no funds.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 1;
Q.e20_kapp_army = 1;
Q.lean_luxemburgist += 1;
Q.crisis_line_3 = 'The Reichswehr: will not march for Kapp';
!}

Leaflets, cigarettes and old comrades from the trenches. In two barracks at Spandau the men elect soldiers' councils and refuse to leave their quarters.

@names
subtitle: Some officers will go into hiding. The rest will settle the account later.
on-arrival: militancy += 5; army_loyalty -= 4; lean_offensive += 1
